    _createStaticVNode("<p class=\"module-kicker\">Module 1 - Foundations</p><h1 id=\"what-ai-is-and-is-not\" tabindex=\"-1\">What AI Is and Is Not <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#what-ai-is-and-is-not\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;What AI Is and Is Not&quot;\">​</a></h1><p class=\"module-lede\">AI can help people draft, organize, compare, and analyze information. It can also produce convincing mistakes. This module builds a realistic mental model before learners use any tool.</p><div class=\"module-meta\"><span><strong>Audience:</strong> All staff</span><span><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> None</span><span><strong>Level:</strong> Beginner</span><span><strong>Evidence:</strong> Task-sort activity</span></div><h2 id=\"learning-objectives\" tabindex=\"-1\">Learning objectives <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#learning-objectives\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Learning objectives&quot;\">​</a></h2><p>By the end of this module, learners can:</p><ul><li>Explain AI in plain language without presenting it as a person or expert.</li><li>Distinguish generative AI, analytics AI, and rules-based automation.</li><li>Identify tasks that are appropriate, inappropriate, or require approval.</li><li>Recognize unsupported content and explain why human review is necessary.</li></ul><h2 id=\"module-content\" tabindex=\"-1\">Module content <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#module-content\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Module content&quot;\">​</a></h2><h3 id=\"_1-ai-as-a-capable-assistant\" tabindex=\"-1\">1. AI as a capable assistant <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#_1-ai-as-a-capable-assistant\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;1. AI as a capable assistant&quot;\">​</a></h3><p>AI is best understood as a tool that supports part of a person&#39;s work. It can organize information, create a first draft, find patterns, or suggest a structure. It does not understand a member&#39;s complete situation, hold professional responsibility, or replace an employee&#39;s judgment.</p><div class=\"tip custom-block\"><p class=\"custom-block-title\">Real-life example</p><p>A program assistant uses an approved AI tool to turn five confirmed training details into a reminder email. The assistant still checks the date, time, audience, and registration link before sending it.</p></div><h3 id=\"_2-what-ai-can-help-with\" tabindex=\"-1\">2. What AI can help with <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#_2-what-ai-can-help-with\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;2. What AI can help with&quot;\">​</a></h3><p>Useful beginner tasks include drafting routine communication, summarizing supplied material, simplifying general information, organizing notes, comparing text, and exploring governed data. These tasks work well when the source is clear and the result can be checked.</p><div class=\"tip custom-block\"><p class=\"custom-block-title\">Real-life example</p><p>A manager has fictional meeting notes containing decisions, actions, and open questions. AI organizes the notes under those three headings, and the manager compares every item with the original notes.</p></div><h3 id=\"_3-what-ai-should-not-be-used-for\" tabindex=\"-1\">3. What AI should not be used for <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#_3-what-ai-should-not-be-used-for\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;3. What AI should not be used for&quot;\">​</a></h3><p>AI should not make final decisions about care, eligibility, crisis response, diagnosis, discipline, or another high-consequence matter requiring authorized professional judgment. It should also not receive information that is prohibited for the selected tool or account.</p><div class=\"tip custom-block\"><p class=\"custom-block-title\">Real-life example</p><p>A care coordinator may use an approved workflow to draft a general follow-up message from permitted facts. The coordinator must not ask AI to determine whether the fictional member qualifies for a service.</p></div><h3 id=\"_4-ai-errors-and-human-responsibility\" tabindex=\"-1\">4. AI errors and human responsibility <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#_4-ai-errors-and-human-responsibility\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;4. AI errors and human responsibility&quot;\">​</a></h3><p>AI can create inaccurate or unsupported statements that sound confident. The employee using the result remains responsible for checking facts, correcting omissions, protecting information, and following the existing approval process.</p><div class=\"tip custom-block\"><p class=\"custom-block-title\">Real-life example</p><p>An AI-written appointment reminder adds a cancellation fee that is absent from the source. The reviewer catches the invented statement, removes it, and verifies the entire message before use.</p></div><h2 id=\"a-practical-definition\" tabindex=\"-1\">A practical definition <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#a-practical-definition\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;A practical definition&quot;\">​</a></h2><p>Artificial intelligence is software that uses patterns in data to perform a task. The task may be creating text, recognizing information, finding a trend, suggesting an answer, or following a learned pattern.</p><p>AI produces an output based on its instructions, available information, design, and configuration. It does not automatically know whether the output is correct, complete, current, permitted, or appropriate for a specific person.</p><div class=\"concept-grid\"><div class=\"concept-card\"><strong>Generative AI</strong><p>Creates new text, images, summaries, or drafts from a prompt.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><strong>Analytics AI</strong><p>Helps find patterns, comparisons, and trends in available data.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><strong>Rules-based automation</strong><p>Performs a defined action when a known condition is met.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><strong>Human judgment</strong><p>Checks context, consequences, permissions, accuracy, and fairness.</p></div></div><h2 id=\"the-human-owned-workflow\" tabindex=\"-1\">The human-owned workflow <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#the-human-owned-workflow\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;The human-owned workflow&quot;\">​</a></h2><div class=\"workflow\"><div class=\"workflow-step\"><b>Input</b><span>A person supplies an instruction and permitted information.</span></div><div class=\"workflow-step\"><b>Processing</b><span>The system identifies patterns and generates a result.</span></div><div class=\"workflow-step\"><b>Output</b><span>The tool returns text, a chart, a suggestion, or another result.</span></div><div class=\"workflow-step\"><b>Review</b><span>A person checks the result against the source and rules.</span></div><div class=\"workflow-step\"><b>Decision</b><span>An authorized person decides what happens next.</span></div></div><p>AI may support one or more steps. It does not inherit the employee&#39;s professional authority or responsibility.</p><h2 id=\"story-the-invented-cancellation-fee\" tabindex=\"-1\">Story: the invented cancellation fee <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#story-the-invented-cancellation-fee\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Story: the invented cancellation fee&quot;\">​</a></h2><p>Jordan needs a friendly appointment reminder. Jordan gives an AI tool the appointment date and time. The draft is clear and professional, but it adds a cancellation fee that was never included in the source.</p><p>The error is easy to miss because the sentence sounds reasonable. This is an example of an <strong>unsupported claim</strong>, sometimes called a hallucination.</p><h3 id=\"think-about-it\" tabindex=\"-1\">Think about it <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#think-about-it\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Think about it&quot;\">​</a></h3><ol><li>What made the draft appear trustworthy?</li><li>What source should Jordan use to check it?</li><li>What could happen if the message were sent without review?</li><li>Which person remains responsible for the final message?</li></ol><h2 id=\"real-life-workplace-uses\" tabindex=\"-1\">Real-life workplace uses <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#real-life-workplace-uses\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Real-life workplace uses&quot;\">​</a></h2><div class=\"use-case-grid\"><div class=\"use-case\"><strong>Drafting</strong><p>Prepare a first draft from approved facts, then review every claim.</p></div><div class=\"use-case\"><strong>Summarizing</strong><p>Shorten supplied information while preserving decisions and open questions.</p></div><div class=\"use-case\"><strong>Explaining</strong><p>Rewrite general information in simpler language without changing its meaning.</p></div><div class=\"use-case\"><strong>Analyzing</strong><p>Explore a governed dataset while checking definitions, filters, and limitations.</p></div></div><div class=\"danger custom-block\"><p class=\"custom-block-title\">AI is not the final authority</p><p>Do not use AI as the final decision-maker for care, eligibility, crisis response, diagnosis, legal interpretation, or another decision that requires authorized professional judgment.</p></div><h2 id=\"interactive-demonstration\" tabindex=\"-1\">Interactive demonstration <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#interactive-demonstration\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Interactive demonstration&quot;\">​</a></h2><p>Give the tool four confirmed facts about a fictional training event. Ask it to create a short reminder. Compare the result with the source facts and label each sentence:</p><ul><li><strong>Supported:</strong> directly matches the source.</li><li><strong>Reworded:</strong> changes presentation but preserves meaning.</li><li><strong>Unsupported:</strong> introduces a new fact or assumption.</li><li><strong>Missing:</strong> leaves out information required for the task.</li></ul><h2 id=\"hands-on-activity-task-sorter\" tabindex=\"-1\">Hands-on activity: task sorter <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#hands-on-activity-task-sorter\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Hands-on activity: task sorter&quot;\">​</a></h2><div class=\"learning-task\"><h3>Make the call</h3><p>Place each task under <strong>Appropriate</strong>, <strong>Approval needed</strong>, or <strong>Not appropriate</strong>. Be ready to explain the permission, privacy, accuracy, or professional-judgment issue behind your answer.</p></div><ol><li>Draft a staff training reminder from five approved facts.</li><li>Decide whether a member qualifies for a service.</li><li>Summarize a fictional meeting transcript.</li><li>Enter real case notes into an unapproved consumer tool.</li><li>Suggest headings for an internal report.</li><li>Determine whether a caller is experiencing a clinical crisis.</li></ol><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\" tabindex=\"-1\">Common mistakes <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#common-mistakes\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Common mistakes&quot;\">​</a></h2><ul><li>Treating fluent language as proof of accuracy.</li><li>Asking AI to decide when it should only assist.</li><li>Assuming the tool understands missing context.</li><li>Using the first output without comparing it with the source.</li><li>Believing a better prompt removes the need for review.</li></ul><h2 id=\"mini-challenge\" tabindex=\"-1\">Mini challenge <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#mini-challenge\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Mini challenge&quot;\">​</a></h2><p>An AI-generated summary says, &quot;The team approved weekly outreach beginning Monday.&quot; The source only says, &quot;The team discussed whether weekly outreach could help.&quot; Rewrite the sentence so it matches the evidence.</p><details class=\"details custom-block\"><summary>Suggested response</summary><p>The team discussed weekly outreach as a possible option. No decision or start date was recorded.</p></details><h2 id=\"knowledge-check\" tabindex=\"-1\">Knowledge check <a class=\"header-anchor\" href=\"#knowledge-check\" aria-label=\"Permalink to &quot;Knowledge check&quot;\">​</a></h2><ol><li>What is the difference between generative AI and rules-based automation?</li><li>Why can an inaccurate output still sound convincing?</li><li>Name two tasks where AI may assist but a person must remain responsible.</li><li>What should happen before an AI-generated message is shared?</li><li>What is an unsupported claim?</li></ol><details class=\"details custom-block\"><summary>Check your answers</summary><ol><li>Generative AI creates a new output; rules-based automation follows predefined conditions and actions. 2. AI creates likely patterns of language and does not independently prove every claim. 3. Examples include drafting communication and summarizing supplied information. 4. Compare it with the approved source, correct it, and obtain required approval. 5. A statement that is not supported by the source or verified evidence.</li></ol></details><div class=\"checkpoint\"><strong>Project evidence:</strong> Choose one fictional workplace task that AI might support. State what the AI would do, what a person must verify, and who owns the final decision.</div>", 50)
